<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hokuriku region</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hokuriku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hokuriku region lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, covering the prefectures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a>, Toyama, Ishikawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a>. Known for heavy winter snow and rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming, it was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, whose leagues, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko-ikki</a>, ruled Kaga province for nearly a century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> much of it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda clan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region.jpg" alt="Hokuriku region" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brent.o.ci · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hokuriku_subregion.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Hokuriku region lies in the north-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, and forms part of the larger Chūbu region. It is almost equivalent to the former Koshi Province and to the Hokurikudō area of pre-modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Its four prefectures are Ishikawa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a> and Toyama, although Niigata is sometimes counted as an addition rather than one of the core prefectures. Other regional definitions overlap it: Hokushin'etsu includes both the Hokuriku and Shin'etsu regions, Kōshin'etsu takes in Niigata, Nagano and Yamanashi, and Shin'etsu covers Niigata and Nagano.</p><p>The region has the highest volume of snowfall of any inhabited and arable region in the world. Dry Siberian air masses pick up high humidity over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> and are forced upwards by the mountains of Honshū, so that the moisture condenses as snow. The long winters and deep snow are depicted in Hokuetsu Seppu, an encyclopedic work of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> describing life in the Uonuma district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata-prefecture">Niigata Prefecture</a>, and in Yasunari Kawabata's novel Snow Country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the Hokuriku was a core recipient of population. It grew to be proportionately much larger than it is today, despite its rural character. In modern times its population has remained consistent, and most urban growth in the 20th century took place instead in Kanto, Chūkyō and Kansai. The region is also known for traditional culture that originated elsewhere and has long since been lost along the Taiheiyō Belt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The main population centres are Jōetsu and Nagaoka, which are special cities, the core cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>, Toyama and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a>, a designated city and the largest, with a population of over 800,000. The main industries include chemicals, medicine, tourism, textiles and textile machinery, heavy machinery, farming and fishing, and Koshihikari, a popular variety of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, is a special product of the region.</p><p>The Hokuriku Shinkansen, formerly the Nagano Shinkansen, was extended from Nagano to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> in March 2015, which gave direct bullet train services from Tokyo and cut the journey from Tokyo to Toyama to about two hours. In March 2024 the line was extended to Tsuruga via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hokuriku region” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the latter half of the late Yayoi, around the 1st century CE, large regional powers emerged across western Japan, including the Tōkai and <b>Hokuriku regions</b>, and by the end of the 2nd century the political entity called Wa-koku had formed.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The preaching of Rennyo spread the True Pure Land, or Ikko, sect among farmers, local warriors and townspeople of the <b>Hokuriku region</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaga-ikko-ikki">Kaga Ikko-ikki</a></li></ul></section>
